WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.140
How does a pushdown automaton

2
00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.920
simulate a grammar? Keep the

3
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.440
unfinished part of a guessed

4
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.480
leftmost derivation on the stack,

5
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.100
expanding variables and matching terminals

6
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.080
to input. We will compile

7
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.020
S becoming zero S one

8
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.900
or epsilon into a machine

9
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.740
and trace zero zero one

10
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.020
one.

11
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.300
Initialize the stack with S

12
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.920
above a fresh dollar marker.

13
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.840
An epsilon move can replace

14
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.120
a top variable by the

15
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.580
right side of any production

16
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.820
for that variable. For our

17
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.720
grammar, the choices are replacing

18
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.500
S by zero S one

19
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.500
or removing S using its

20
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.600
epsilon production.

21
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.780
The push-string shorthand must leave

22
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.600
the first right-side symbol on

23
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.840
top. To push zero S

24
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.640
one using single-symbol operations, push

25
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.540
one first, then S, then

26
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.940
zero. Pushing in written order

27
00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.900
would reverse the intended unfinished

28
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.760
word and would not simulate

29
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:55.340
the grammar.

30
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.940
From stack zero S one

31
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.900
dollar, read the first zero

32
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.640
and pop that same terminal.

33
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.180
The remaining stack is S

34
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.660
one dollar. The invariant says

35
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.020
the consumed prefix followed by

36
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.020
the stack’s unfinished portion is

37
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.500
a sentential form of a

38
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.440
guessed derivation.

39
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.580
Expand the remaining S once

40
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.140
more using the wrapping rule.

41
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.000
Because a one was already

42
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.660
below it, the new stack

43
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.220
is zero S one one

44
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.280
dollar. Read and pop the

45
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.460
next zero, leaving S one

46
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.200
one dollar while the unread

47
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.740
input is one one.

48
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.060
Now choose the epsilon production

49
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.820
for S. Removing the variable

50
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.700
leaves two terminal ones on

51
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.840
the stack, exactly the unread

52
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.320
suffix. Match and pop each

53
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.180
one, exposing the bottom marker

54
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.380
with no input left.

55
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.600
The final move pops the

56
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.120
marker and enters the accepting

57
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.140
state. It succeeds as an

58
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.180
accepting computation only after the

59
00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.780
entire input has been read.

60
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.040
Replacing the marker test by

61
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.100
epsilon pop would allow leaving

62
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.160
unmatched generated terminals on the

63
00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:57.520
stack.

64
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.660
The tutorial’s wrong construction accepts

65
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.100
zero zero one even though

66
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.360
a generated one remains unmatched.

67
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.860
Its final epsilon, epsilon to

68
00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.620
epsilon arrow ignores the stack

69
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.720
entirely and can fire anyway.

70
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.340
The dollar-marker condition prevents that

71
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.520
false acceptance, rather than merely

72
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.820
decorating the diagram.

73
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.480
For correctness, an accepting machine

74
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.620
run records a successful sequence

75
00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.620
of grammar expansions and terminal

76
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.600
matches, so its input is

77
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.220
generated. Conversely, follow a leftmost

78
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.300
derivation and choose exactly its

79
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.080
productions to obtain an accepting

80
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.440
branch. Other branches may get

81
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.800
stuck or expand forever, which

82
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.800
is harmless for existential recognition.

83
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.660
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.400
Distinguish variable expansion from terminal

85
00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.580
matching, use reverse primitive push

86
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.440
order, and keep the consumed-prefix

87
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.860
plus unfinished-stack invariant. Acceptance needs

88
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.660
both input exhaustion and the

89
00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.820
marker-based finish; a guessed production

90
00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.980
is not knowledge of the

91
00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.880
correct derivation.
